[Event "Benoni: White's setup, and the gambit refuted"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/3Ll0yprL/yHaXNbKj"]

{ We face the Benoni with White. One setup answers it: d5, knight c3, e4, h3, knight f3, bishop d3. Queen pawn first. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 (1. e4 { The king pawn. Fine chess, but the Benoni only appears after the queen pawn. } 1... c5 { That is the Sicilian, a different world. Today, d4. }) 1... Nf6 { He brings the knight first. Now the c pawn: two pawns in the middle, the Benoni invitation. [%cal Yc2c4] } 2. c4 (2. Nf3 { Sound, but a different order. We play c4 first and keep every game alike. } 2... c5 3. d5 e6 { Still a Benoni, but he has extra options with no pawn on c4. Play c4 first. }) (2. Bf4 { The London. A whole other study in this list. } 2... c5 3. e3 { Fine chess, but not the Benoni fight we are learning today. }) 2... c5 { The Benoni: he hits d4 from the side. Do not take, do not defend. Push past: pawn to d5, the wedge. [%cal Yd4d5] } 3. d5 { The wedge on d5 cramps him. Everything else in this opening flows from it. [%csl Gd5] } (3. dxc5 { He wins the pawn back at once, and all your space is gone. } 3... e6 4. Nf3 Bxc5 { A level game. Push past instead and keep the wedge. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (3. Nf3 { Fine, but he trades and the Benoni is gone. } 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 { Open and level. The wedge on d5 is our whole plan. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (3. e3 { Too modest. } 3... cxd4 4. exd4 d5 { He gets his own share of the centre and no cramp at all. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (3. Nc3 { Natural, but the pawn on d4 hangs. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { Your queen is out early and he develops on her. [%cal Rc6d4] }) 3... e6 { He bites at the wedge. Knight to c3 first: it guards d5, so you can retake with the pawn. [%cal Yb1c3] } 4. Nc3 (4. Nf3 { Also good, and usually the same game. Knight c3 first is our order: it guards d5. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 { The same house by another door. }) (4. dxe6 { Never. You open his f column and hand him the centre. } 4... fxe6 5. Nc3 d5 { Two pawns in the middle for him, and your wedge is gone. [%csl Rd5,Re6] }) (4. e4 { The pawn goes one move too early: nothing guards it. } 4... Nxe4 { A pawn for free. Knight c3 first, then e4. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (4. g3 { The fianchetto system. Sound, but a different plan. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Bg2 { Good chess. Ours puts the pawn on e4 and the bishop on d3. }) (4. Bf4 { Playable, another order. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 { Fine. Ours brings the knight to c3 first because it guards d5. }) 4... exd5 { He takes. Retake with the c pawn, never the knight: the pawn on d5 is the wedge. [%cal Yc4d5] } 5. cxd5 (5. Nxd5 { The knight retakes, and he trades it off at once. } 5... Nxd5 6. cxd5 d6 { The wedge is there, but your best knight is gone and his game is easier. }) (5. Qxd5 { Look again: his knight on f6 covers d5. } 5... Nxd5 { The queen is gone. [%cal Rf6d5] }) 5... d6 { He seals d6. Now e4: a big centre, and it guards d5 forever. [%cal Ye2e4] } 6. e4 (6. Nf3 { Just as good. } 6... g6 7. e4 Bg7 8. h3 { The same house by another door. Ours plays e4 first, while nothing can stop it. }) (6. f4 { The Four Pawns Attack. Very sharp, and a different lesson. } 6... g6 7. e4 Bg7 { His bishop bites on your big centre. Our setup is calmer. }) (6. e3 { One square short. } 6... g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 { Fine, but the pawn on e4 is what guards d5 and gains space. [%cal Ge2e4] }) (6. Bf4 { The bishop hits d6, but a knight chases it at once. } 6... Nh5 7. Be3 Nf6 { Two moves each and nothing has changed, except e4 is still not in. }) 6... g6 { The fianchetto: his bishop will burn down the long diagonal. Play h3 now, before anything pins on g4. [%cal Yh2h3] } 7. h3 { A small move with a big job: the knight will go to f3, and nothing will ever pin it. [%csl Gg4] } (7. Nf3 { Also fine. } 7... Bg7 8. h3 { The same house. Our order only shuts the g4 square one move sooner. }) (7. f4 { The Four Pawns idea, one move later. Sharp and playable, but a different fight. } 7... Bg7 8. Bb5+ Nfd7 { Both sides have chances. Ours keeps the pawns calm. }) (7. Bd3 { The right square, a move too soon. } 7... Bg7 8. Nf3 Bg4 { The pin. It is not deadly, but the h pawn move rules it out for good. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (7. Bb5+ { The check that asks his knight where to go. Playable. } 7... Nbd7 8. Nf3 Bg7 { A fair game. Ours keeps the bishop for d3. }) (7. Bg5 { Playable, but the bishop is a target. } 7... Bg7 8. Bd3 h6 9. Bf4 Nh5 { Chased twice already. }) 7... Bg7 { Now the knight to f3, safe from any pin. [%cal Yg1f3] } 8. Nf3 (8. Bd3 { Also good. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 { The same house by another door. }) (8. Be3 { The engine likes this too. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 { Sound, a different setup. Ours brings the knight and bishop first. }) (8. Bb5+ { A check that helps him: a6 and b5 come with tempo. } 8... Nbd7 9. Nf3 a6 10. Bd3 b5 { His queen side is rolling and your bishop got kicked home. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (8. Bg5 { Playable, though the bishop can be hit by h6 later. } 8... O-O 9. Bd3 Re8 { A fair game. Ours brings the knight and castles first. }) (8. Be2 { Playable, but on e2 the bishop does not guard e4, and e4 is what he attacks. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 b5 { Now his b5 gambit is a fair one: nothing guards e4. [%cal Rf6e4] }) 8... O-O { He castles. The bishop to d3: it guards e4 and looks at his king side. [%cal Yf1d3] } 9. Bd3 { The setup is complete. Castle next, and then look at d6, his weak spot, all game long. [%csl Rd6] } (9. Be3 { The engine's own favourite; a fine move. } 9... Re8 10. Nd2 { Solid chess, a different setup. Ours has the bishop on d3 every game. }) (9. Nd2 { Also good: the knight heads to c4 to lean on d6. } 9... b6 10. Be2 Ba6 { Playable, another plan. }) (9. Bg5 { Playable, but the bishop invites the rook to e8 and c4 with tempo. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 c4 { He gains space and time. }) (9. Be2 { Playable, but on e2 the bishop does not guard e4. } 9... b5 10. Bxb5 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 { His gambit works: he gets the piece back. A fight. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (9. Bb5 { The bishop on b5 walks into a fork. } 9... Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Qa5+ 11. Nc3 Bxc3+ 12. bxc3 Qxb5 { A pawn gone and your pawns wrecked. [%cal Ra5b5,Ra5e1] }) (9. Bc4 { The bishop looks bold, but it stands in front of his pawns. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 c4 11. Bc2 { Three bishop moves to reach c2, while he gained the rook column and c4. }) 9... b5 { The Benoni gambit! He offers the b pawn to rip open your centre. Take it with the knight. [%cal Yc3b5] } 10. Nxb5 { The bishop stays on d3 and keeps guarding e4. He will hit e4 anyway; we are ready. [%csl Ge4] } (10. Bxb5 { The other capture. Also good, but wild: your bishop and e4 both hang. } 10... Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 13. Nxd6 { A messy fight. The knight capture keeps the bishop home. }) (10. O-O { Declining is possible. } 10... b4 11. Na4 Ba6 { His pawns roll and your knight sits on the rim. Take the pawn. [%csl Ra4] }) (10. a4 { You attack the pawn; he pushes it. } 10... b4 11. Ne2 Re8 { His pawns and pieces flow, and e4 is a target. [%csl Rb4][%cal Rf6e4] }) (10. Nd2 { Passive. } 10... c4 11. Bc2 b4 { The knight is chased and his pawns own the queen side. }) (10. e5 { The break comes too early. } 10... dxe5 11. Bxb5 e4 { Your knight is hit, and the game is level at best. }) 10... Re8 { The best reply: the rook eyes e4 first. Castle, and let the pawn hang: a rook on e1 will pin his knight. [%cal Ye1g1] } 11. O-O (11. Nc3 { The knight goes back, and he takes on e4 anyway. } 11... Nxe4 12. Nxe4 f5 { The pawn comes back to him with tempo. Castle instead. }) (11. Bg5 { Castle first. } 11... c4 12. Bxc4 Rxe4+ { With the king still on e1, his rook comes in with check. [%cal Re4e1] }) (11. Nd2 { The engine likes this too: the knight heads to c4. } 11... Nxd5 12. Nc4 { He grabs d5, you lean on d6. A fair fight, but a different one. }) (11. Nxd6 { The pawn is guarded by his queen. } 11... Qxd6 { A knight for a pawn. [%cal Rd8d6] }) 11... Nxe4 { He takes. Do not rush: rook to e1, and his knight on e4 is pinned to the rook on e8. [%cal Yf1e1] } 12. Re1 (12. Bxe4 { Also fine: you take first and chase the rook. } 12... Rxe4 13. Bg5 Qd7 14. Nc3 Re8 { Level material. Playable, but the rook pin keeps more tension. }) (12. Nd2 { A trade, and the pressure is gone. } 12... Nxd2 13. Bxd2 a6 { Dead level, and his bishops are happy. }) (12. Nxd6 { His knight on e4 guards d6. } 12... Nxd6 { A knight for a pawn. [%cal Re4d6] }) 12... a6 { He kicks your knight. Back to c3, and look: it hits his pinned knight on e4. [%cal Yb5c3] } 13. Nc3 (13. Na3 { Also good, though the knight is on the rim. } 13... Nf6 14. Rxe8+ Nxe8 15. Bg5 { Playable; the knight comes back via c4 later. }) (13. Rxe4 { You take first. } 13... Rxe4 14. Nxd6 Rb4 { Messy but level. The knight retreat is simpler. }) (13. Ng5 { The knight jumps at e4, but yours on b5 hangs and f2 is loose. } 13... Nxf2 { A messy fight, no longer in your favour. Retreat the knight instead. }) (13. Bxe4 { You win his knight, but your own knight on b5 hangs. } 13... axb5 { Level again, and his pawns are healthier. Retreat first. }) 13... Nxc3 { He trades. Take on e8 with check first, then recapture on c3. The order matters. [%cal Ye1e8] } 14. Rxe8+ (14. bxc3 { Also fine. } 14... Bxc3 15. Rxe8+ Qxe8 16. Rb1 { He took c3; you get the b column and a lead in play. Playable. }) (14. Qd2 { The queen steps out, and his knight simply steps back. } 14... Nb5 { You are a whole knight down. [%cal Rc3b5] }) (14. Bg5 { Look at c3: his knight attacks your queen. } 14... Nxd1 { He takes it first. [%cal Rc3d1] }) 14... Qxe8 { Now take back on c3 with the pawn. Your pawns on c3 and d5 form a chain. [%cal Yb2c3] } 15. bxc3 { Material is level again: his b pawn for your e pawn. The b column is now yours. [%csl Gb1] } (15. Qd2 { The queen attacks the knight, but it runs with a pawn. } 15... Nxd5 { A pawn down, and your wedge is gone. [%cal Rc3d5] }) (15. Qb3 { Same story: the knight simply steps away. } 15... Nb5 { A whole knight up for him. [%cal Rc3b5] }) 15... Nd7 { His knight heads for e5. Rook to b1: the open column, and it eyes b7 behind his bishop. [%cal Ya1b1] } 16. Rb1 (16. Bd2 { Also good: the bishop guards c3 first. } 16... Bb7 17. Qb3 { Fine chess; the rook comes to b1 a move later. }) (16. Bf4 { The bishop hits d6, but leaves c3 alone. } 16... Bxc3 17. Rc1 Be5 { He won a pawn with tempo. [%cal Rg7c3] }) (16. Bg5 { Same problem. } 16... Bxc3 17. Rb1 Ne5 { A pawn gone, and his pieces are the active ones. }) (16. Ba3 { The bishop eyes d6, but c3 falls. } 16... Bxc3 17. Rb1 Ne5 { A pawn down again. [%cal Rg7c3] }) 16... Ne5 { He offers a trade. Take it: his knight on e5 was his best piece, and it hits your bishop. [%cal Yf3e5] } 17. Nxe5 (17. Be4 { The bishop steps aside, and he takes anyway. } 17... Nxf3+ 18. Bxf3 Bxc3 { The c3 pawn falls. [%cal Rg7c3] }) (17. Bc2 { The bishop hides, but the knight takes on f3 and his queen comes in. } 17... Nxf3+ 18. Qxf3 Qe1+ 19. Kh2 Be5+ { The checks win the c3 pawn next. [%cal Re1e5] }) (17. Nd2 { The knight retreats and blocks your own queen. } 17... Nxd3 { A whole bishop. [%cal Re5d3] }) 17... Bxe5 { Now the bishop to d2: it guards c3, and your queen and rook are free to work the b column. [%cal Yc1d2] } 18. Bd2 { Level material. You have the b column and the d5 wedge against his weak d6 pawn; he has the bishops. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gb1b7] } (18. Qb3 { Also good: queen and rook double on the b column. } 18... Ra7 19. Qb8 { Playable; the bishop move first keeps c3 safe. }) (18. c4 { Playable: the pawn is fixed and safe. } 18... Bd7 19. Bh6 { Level, but d4 is now his square forever. [%csl Rd4] }) (18. Bh6 { Active, but c3 hangs. } 18... Bxc3 19. Rb6 Be5 { A pawn down. [%cal Re5c3] }) (18. Bg5 { Same problem. } 18... Bxc3 19. Qf3 Bd4 { A pawn down again. [%cal Re5c3] }) (18. Bf4 { Nothing guards f4. } 18... Bxf4 { A whole bishop. [%cal Re5f4] }) 1-0


[Event "Benoni: he takes on e4 as well"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
