[FEN "rnbq1rk1/p4pbp/3p1np1/1NpP4/4P3/3B1N1P/PP3PP1/R1BQK2R b KQ - 0 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbq1rk1/p4pbp/3p1np1/1NpP4/4P3/3B1N1P/PP3PP1/R1BQK2R_b_KQ_-_0_10"]

{ You took on b5. Now he grabs e4 too: a knight for two pawns and open lines. Not sound, but you must find the answers. }
10... Nxe4 { The knight lands on e4 and attacks your bishop. What do you play? } 11. Bxe4 { Simplest: take the knight. Now his rook comes to e8 and pins your bishop to the king. } (11. O-O { You castle and let him choose. } 11... a6 12. Na3 Nf6 { He hits your knight and then brings his own home: level, and the piece was yours for the taking. [%cal Ga6b5] }) (11. Nxd6 { The d6 pawn was guarded. } 11... Nxd6 { A knight for a pawn. [%cal Re4d6] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight comes back, but his knight is still on e4, and it takes. } 11... Nxc3 12. bxc3 Bxc3+ { The bishop forks king and rook. [%cal Rc3e1,Rc3a1] }) (11. Qe2 { The queen attacks the knight, but he attacks yours. } 11... a6 12. Bxe4 axb5 { Level, and his pawns roll. Just take the knight at once. }) 11... Re8 { The pin: your bishop cannot leave the e column and nothing can shield it. What defends it? } 12. Ng5 { The knight guards e4 and eyes f7 and e6. His h pawn will kick it. } (12. Nd2 { Also holds the bishop. Playable, but the knight on g5 has teeth. } 12... Ba6 13. a4 { A slower fight; you keep the extra piece for now. }) (12. O-O { You give the bishop back. } 12... Rxe4 13. Bg5 Qd7 { Level material and his pieces are active. The knight to g5 kept everything. }) (12. Qc2 { The queen guards e4, but his bishop hits it too. } 12... Bf5 13. Ng5 Bxe4 14. Nxe4 f5 { The piece is gone again; level. }) (12. Qe2 { The queen guards e4, but his bishop pins your knight to her. } 12... Ba6 13. a4 Qa5+ { The extra piece is slipping away and your king is in the centre. }) 12... h6 { He kicks the knight. Retreat, or jump forward? } 13. Ne6 { Into his camp. If the f pawn takes, d takes e6, the bishop on e4 stares at a8 and the pawn chokes him. [%cal Ge4a8] } (13. Nf3 { Back home, and the bishop is lost. } 13... Rxe4 { Nothing guards e4 now. [%cal Re8e4] }) (13. Nxf7 { A tempting hit. } 13... Rxe4+ 14. Kf1 Kxf7 { He takes your bishop with check, then the knight. You are worse. }) (13. O-O { You leave the knight to die. } 13... hxg5 14. Bc2 { A piece gone for hope. }) 13... Qa5+ { A check, and the queen also attacks your knight on b5. Block, and with what? } 14. Nc3 { The only block that saves everything: the knight on b5 comes home with tempo. He must trade on c3. } (14. Bd2 { The block loses the other knight. } 14... Qxb5 { Gone. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (14. Qd2 { Same problem. } 14... Qxb5 { The b5 knight was undefended. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (14. Kf1 { The king steps, and the knight falls with check. } 14... Qxb5+ 15. Bd3 Qxd3+ 16. Qxd3 Ba6 { Queens come off and he is fine. Block with the knight instead. }) 14... Bxc3+ { He takes with check. Take back. } 15. bxc3 (15. Bd2 { A block, but everything trades and your knight on e6 is left to die. } 15... Bxd2+ 16. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 fxe6 { A piece down. }) (15. Kf1 { The king steps aside and your rook on h1 sleeps. } 15... Be5 { His bishop is a monster and your king is stuck. [%csl Rf1] }) 15... Qxc3+ { Check again, and the rook on a1 hangs. Block. } 16. Bd2 { The bishop blocks with tempo: it hits his queen. } (16. Qd2 { Trading queens is possible, but why? } 16... Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 fxe6 { A small edge. The bishop block keeps far more. }) (16. Kf1 { The king steps and the rook on a1 falls. } 16... Qxa1 { A whole rook. [%cal Rc3a1] }) (16. Ke2 { Into the fire. } 16... Ba6+ { Check, and the rook on a1 falls next. [%cal Ra6e2] }) 16... Qc4 { His queen hits your bishop on e4. What is the cleanest answer? } 17. Qe2 { Offer the trade. He cannot refuse without losing the queen. } (17. Qf3 { Also good: the queen guards the bishop and hits f7. } 17... Bxe6 18. Rc1 { His queen is in trouble too. A fine fight, still yours. }) (17. Bf3 { The bishop steps back; playable. } 17... fxe6 { He takes the knight and you take back with the pawn next. A smaller edge. }) (17. Qc2 { The other trade offer. } 17... Qxc2 18. Bxc2 fxe6 { Also playable, a bit less. }) (17. Nc7 { The fork looks great, but his rook takes your bishop with check first. } 17... Rxe4+ { And now the fork is gone. [%cal Re8e4] }) (17. Nxc5 { The knight grabs a pawn and dies. } 17... dxc5 { A knight for a pawn. [%cal Rd6c5] }) (17. Bxg6 { The sacrifice is not there. } 17... Bxe6 18. dxe6 fxg6 { A bishop for two pawns and nothing else. }) 17... Qxe2+ { Queens off. } 18. Kxe2 { He will take the knight and your d pawn takes back: a pawn on e6 in his throat, and his king side full of holes. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gd5e6] } 1-0


