{ Your knight is on g5. This time he attacks your pinned bishop with the f pawn. A mistake, if you find the answers. }
12... f5 { The pawn hits your bishop, and the bishop is pinned to the king. What breaks the pin? } 13. O-O { Castling. The bishop is free, and the e column will belong to your rook. } (13. Qb3 { The queen leaves the column, but the bishop still falls. } 13... fxe4 14. O-O a6 15. Nc3 { Still better, but his pawn reached e4 with tempo. Castle first. }) (13. Qa4 { An attack on the rook, but the pin stays. } 13... Bd7 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Rxe6 { A pawn for the pinned bishop. Roughly level. }) (13. a4 { Too slow. } 13... fxe4 14. O-O h6 { He wins the bishop and keeps everything. Level. }) (13. Qc2 { The queen guards the bishop, but a check comes. } 13... Qa5+ 14. Nc3 Qa6 { Your king is stuck and the bishop still pinned. Level. }) (13. Qe2 { The queen guards the bishop, but the pin stays. } 13... fxe4 14. O-O e3 { Level, and the pawn on e3 is a thorn. }) (13. Ne6 { The fork square, one move too early. } 13... Qa5+ 14. Nc3 Bxe6 { He takes the knight with the check in hand. Level at best. }) (13. Qf3 { The queen guards the bishop, and he kicks the knight. } 13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Rxe6 { Level, and the pin still stands. }) (13. Nxd6 { The pawn on d6 was guarded by his queen. } 13... Qxd6 { A knight gone. [%cal Rd8d6] }) 13... fxe4 { He takes. How do you attack the e4 pawn? } 14. Re1 { The rook joins: the pawn on e4 is attacked, and the pin on the e column is now yours. } (14. Bf4 { Also good: hitting d6 first. } 14... Be5 15. Qd2 Bf5 16. Bxe5 Rxe5 { Fine for you, but the rook on e1 is the sharper way. }) (14. Qb3 { The queen comes out, but he has time. } 14... a6 15. Nc3 Bf5 { About a pawn better; the rook on e1 wins more. }) (14. Ne6 { The fork square, still too early. } 14... Bxe6 15. dxe6 Na6 { Half of what the rook gives. }) (14. Nxe4 { The knight takes and dies. } 14... Rxe4 { Nothing guards e4. [%cal Re8e4] }) (14. Nc3 { The knight comes back, but e4 stands. } 14... Bf5 { His pawn on e4 is solid now and he is fine. }) 14... Bf5 { He guards the pawn. Which piece of yours has not moved yet? } 15. Bf4 { The bishop hits d6. He must guard it with the bishop, and the bishop leaves g7. } (15. g4 { Also strong: the pawn kicks his bishop. } 15... a6 16. Nc3 { Still winning, but sharper than it needs to be. }) (15. Ne6 { The knight jumps in. } 15... Bxe6 16. dxe6 d5 { You keep an edge, but the pawn on e6 will fall. Less. }) (15. Nc3 { The knight comes back to attack e4. } 15... Bxc3 16. bxc3 { Half of your edge is gone with the doubled pawns. }) (15. Nxe4 { Still not. } 15... Bxe4 16. Rxe4 Rxe4 { A piece gone. [%cal Re8e4] }) (15. Rxe4 { The rook takes and the bishop takes back. } 15... Bxe4 16. Nxe4 { Now his rook takes the knight. A rook for nothing. [%cal Re8e4] }) 15... Be5 { The bishop guards d6. Count on e4 now. } 16. Nxe4 { The knight takes. It is guarded by the rook, and it eyes f6 and d6. } (16. Qd2 { Slower. } 16... Nd7 17. Nxe4 Bxe4 18. Rxe4 { Still good, but he has developed. }) (16. Bxe5 { The trade helps him. } 16... Rxe5 17. f4 exf3 18. Nxf3 { A smaller edge; keep the bishops on. }) (16. Rxe4 { The rook takes and the bishop takes back. } 16... Bxe4 17. Nxe4 { An exchange down, and his bishop takes on f4 next. }) (16. Nc3 { The knight comes back and forgets the bishop. } 16... Bxf4 { A bishop gone. [%cal Re5f4] }) 16... Bxf4 { He takes the bishop and expects you to take back. Is there something better? } 17. Nf6+ { Check, and a fork: the rook on e8 stands behind the king. He must take with the queen. } (17. Qf3 { The queen hits both bishops, but he takes the knight. } 17... Rxe4 18. Rxe4 Be5 { You are worse. }) (17. Nbxd6 { The knight grabs d6. } 17... Bxd6 { And is taken. [%cal Rf4d6] }) (17. Nc7 { The fork on a8 and e8 comes second. } 17... Rxe4 18. Rxe4 Bxe4 { He takes two pieces first. }) (17. Ng3 { The knight runs and everything hangs. } 17... Rxe1+ 18. Qxe1 Bd7 { A piece down. }) 17... Qxf6 { The queen takes. Now the rook on e8 is loose. } 18. Rxe8+ { Check. } (18. Nc7 { The other fork, one move too late. } 18.