4 can wait for a6. Less. } ) ( 5. Bf4 { Develops. } O-O 6. e4 { Playable, but the bishop is out early. Less. } ) ( 5. Bg5 { Pins nothing. } d6 6. e4 { The bishop is out early and h6, g5 can chase it. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. e3 { Timid. } O-O 6. Nf3 { The pawn should go to e4 in one step. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. g3 { Fianchettoes. } O-O 6. Bg2 { Playable, but the bishop belongs on d3 here. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. f4 { Space. } O-O 6. Nf3 { The f pawn goes early and e4 is loose behind it. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. f3 { Prepares e4. } O-O { The pawn takes f3 from the knight. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Nh3 { Develops. } O-O { The knight is on the rim. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Qb3 { Guards c4. } O-O { The queen is out for no reason. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Qd2 { Connects. } O-O { The queen is out for no reason. Level. } ) ( 5. Qa4 { Checks nothing. } O-O { The queen is out for no reason. Level. } ) ( 5. Nb5 { Jumps at c7. } Ne4 { The knight left c3: his knight lands on e4 and a check on a5 is coming. Worse. [%cal Rf6e4] } ) O-O { He castles. Now the usual precaution before the knight comes out. } 6. h3 { The h pawn: no pin on g4, no knight jump either. } ( 6. Nf3 { Develops. } d6 7. Be2 { Also fine: the pin on g4 is met by the bishop on e2. } ) ( 6. Be2 { Develops. } d6 7. Nf3 { Also fine: the same setup, the bishop a little more modest. } ) ( 6. Bd3 { Develops. } d6 7. Nge2 { Also fine: the knight goes to e2 and h3 can wait. } ) ( 6. Be3 { Develops. } d6 7. Nf3 { Playable, though the bishop can wait. Less. } ) ( 6. Nge2 { Develops. } d6 7. Ng3 { Playable, but the knight does more from f3. Less. } ) ( 6. Qc2 { Guards e4. } d6 7. Nf3 { Playable, though the queen is early. Less. } ) ( 6. e5 { The break. } Ne8 { The pawn is overextended: d6 comes and it must be traded. Less. } ) ( 6. Bg5 { Pins nothing. } d6 7. Nf3 { The bishop is out early and h6, g5 can chase it. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. Bf4 { Hits d6. } d6 7. Nf3 { The bishop has no target. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. f3 { Guards e4. } d6 7. Nge2 { The pawn takes f3 from the knight. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. a4 { Stops b5. } d6 7. Nf3 { The a pawn goes before b5 is a threat. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. f4 { Space. } d6 7. Nf3 { The f pawn goes early and e4 is loose behind it. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. Bd2 { Develops. } d6 { The bishop does nothing on d2. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. Qd2 { Connects. } d6 { The queen is out for no reason. Level. } ) ( 6. Qe2 { Guards e4. } d6 { The queen blocks the bishop on f1. Level. } ) ( 6. g3 { Fianchettoes. } d6 7. Bg2 { The bishop belongs on d3 here, and the dark squares loosen. Level. } ) ( 6. Qf3 { Guards e4. } d6 { The queen sits where the knight belongs. Level. } ) ( 6. Nb5 { Jumps at c7. } Nxe4 { The e pawn falls, and c7 is guarded by the queen. Worse. [%cal Rf6e4] } ) d6 { He holds the centre with the pawn. Develop the knight. } 7. Nf3 { The knight to f3, safe from the pin. } ( 7. Bd3 { Develops. } e6 8. Nf3 { Also fine: the same setup in another order. } ) ( 7. Be3 { Develops. } Na6 8. Nf3 { Also fine, though the bishop can wait. } ) ( 7. Be2 { Develops. } e6 { Playable, though d3 is the better square. Less. } ) ( 7. a4 { Stops b5. } Re8 { The a pawn goes before b5 is a threat. Less. } ) ( 7. Qc2 { Guards e4. } e6 { He opens the centre and the queen is early. Half the edge. } ) ( 7. Nge2 { Develops. } e6 { He opens the centre: the knight does more from f3. Half the edge. } ) ( 7. Bg5 { Pins. } e6 { He opens the centre and the pin bites nothing. Half the edge. } ) ( 7. Bf4 { Hits d6. } e6 { He opens the centre: the bishop has no target. Half the edge. } ) ( 7. Bd2 { Develops. } e6 { The bishop does nothing on d2. Level. } ) ( 7. g4 { Grabs space. } e6 { The king side is loosened for nothing. Level. } ) ( 7. g3 { Fianchettoes. } e6 { The dark squares loosen around your king. Level. } ) ( 7. Qd2 { Connects. } e6 { The queen is out for no reason. Level. } ) ( 7. f4 { Space. } e6 { The f pawn goes early and e4 is loose behind it. Level. } ) ( 7. Qf3 { Guards e4. } 